<section class="band why-mos" aria-labelledby="why-mos-title">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="why-mos-layout">
      <div class="why-mos-heading">
        <span class="mos-eyebrow">Why MOS exists</span>
        <h2 class="mos-section-title" id="why-mos-title">Easy self-hosting, without giving up ownership</h2>
        <p class="mos-body-lg mos-meta">
          MOS is not the first project to make open-source applications installable from a catalog. It exists
          because the projects that came before it each solved a different part of the problem.
        </p>
        <a class="why-mos-link" href="/docs/why-your-own-cloud/">
          Why ownership matters
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-arrow-right" /></svg>
        </a>
      </div>

      <div class="why-mos-story">
        <div class="why-mos-chapter">
          <span class="why-mos-project">TrueNAS SCALE</span>
          <p>
            pairs excellent app discovery with a powerful NAS-first system. That power also brings storage pools,
            permissions, networking, and other infrastructure choices into installing an app—a natural fit for
            technical operators, but a high bar for everyday use.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="why-mos-chapter">
          <span class="why-mos-project">CasaOS</span>
          <p>
            showed how friendly a home server could be. Its release pace later slowed as its maker’s visible focus
            moved toward ZimaOS and hardware, reinforcing the value of software that remains independently usable
            and maintainable when a product direction changes.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="why-mos-chapter">
          <span class="why-mos-project">Cloudron</span>
          <p>
            demonstrates how polished self-hosted app management can become. Its core platform moved from open
            source to source-available, while demand remains for a free and fully open-source Cloudron alternative.
          </p>
        </div>
        <p class="why-mos-answer">
          <strong>MOS brings those lessons together:</strong> an app-first private cloud for ordinary hardware,
          open all the way down, with no project account or license service required to keep it running. It is still
          an early beta—and an invitation to
          <a class="mos-link" href="https://discord.gg/YMpF6faBCv">help shape the alternative</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
